CONTEXT=urn:cts:latinLit:phi0474.phi016.omar:2.2.t37-urn:cts:latinLit:phi0474.phi016.omar:2.2.t55
REASONING=The sentence has two coordinated independent verbs, "habent" (transitive active, with "artes" as subject and "vinclum" as direct object) and "continentur" (transitive passive, sharing the same subject "artes"), joined by "et" — so "et" relates to both verbs ("habent", "continentur") as a coordinating conjunction. Embedded in the subject phrase "omnes artes" is a relative clause "quae ad humanitatem pertinent": "quae" is the relative pronoun (relatedtoken1 -> antecedent "artes", relatedtoken2 -> subject of "pertinent"), and "pertinent" is dependent, with its unit-verb relation into "quae". "pertinent" is intransitive; "ad humanitatem" is the adverbial prepositional phrase modifying it. "artes" takes a single 'subject' relation, assigned to the first of the two coordinated verbs ("habent"), since only one relation slot pair is available and it is already used by nothing else. "etenim" is a connective particle; rather than a true coordinating conjunction joining two items within the sentence, it introduces the whole sentence, so I treat it as adverbial to the first main verb "habent". This is a mild judgment call: one could alternatively treat it as a one-sided coordinating conjunction (relatedtoken1 -> "habent" only); I chose the adverbial reading because "etenim" functions as an explanatory particle ('for indeed') rather than coordinating two parallel elements. "quasi" ('as it were') qualifies the ablative expression; I attach it adverbially to "continentur". "cognatione" is an ablative (of means) depending on "continentur", with "quadam" adjectival to it; "inter se" is an adverbial prepositional phrase on "continentur".

